WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.069
Decision science helps people make clearer, more explicit,

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.296
and more reviewable choices under uncertainty.

00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:08.264
It is not a promise of perfect prediction.

00:00:08.264 --> 00:00:14.202
It is a discipline for improving the quality of the decision before the outcome is known,

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:16.429
then learning from what happens afterward.

00:00:16.429 --> 00:00:19.027
Strong decision practice starts with the frame.

00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:21.996
Define the real choice, not just the symptom.

00:00:21.996 --> 00:00:25.336
Clarify the scope, constraints, stakeholders, time horizon, decision owner,

00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:26.820
and consequences of inaction.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.304
Then clarify objectives, values,

00:00:28.304 --> 00:00:32.758
and criteria so the group knows what it is trying to optimize

00:00:32.758 --> 00:00:35.727
and what tradeoffs it is willing to accept.

00:00:35.727 --> 00:00:39.438
Good decision makers generate realistic options and avoid false binaries.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:42.778
They compare tradeoffs, opportunity costs, reversibility, and staged commitments.

00:00:42.778 --> 00:00:46.860
They make uncertainty explicit with probabilities, base rates, forecasts, confidence ranges,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.458
scenario thinking, and updates as evidence changes.

00:00:49.458 --> 00:00:53.911
They consider risk as likelihood, impact, context, downside, upside, tail exposure, mitigation,

00:00:53.911 --> 00:00:54.653
and contingency.

00:00:54.653 --> 00:00:57.251
They also respect the limits of judgment.

00:00:57.251 --> 00:01:00.591
Bias and noise are normal risks, not personal insults.

00:01:00.591 --> 00:01:03.560
Checklists, pre-mortems, red teams, independent estimates, decision records,

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:08.013
and structured review help teams improve without pretending people are perfectly rational.

00:01:08.013 --> 00:01:08.756
In teams,

00:01:08.756 --> 00:01:13.209
role clarity and psychological safety make it more likely that the right

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:15.807
information reaches the decision maker in time.

00:01:15.807 --> 00:01:19.147
The goal is better judgment, better process, better learning,

00:01:19.147 --> 00:01:21.373
and more reliable action over time.

00:01:21.373 --> 00:01:24.342
You will still make decisions with incomplete information.

00:01:24.342 --> 00:01:27.311
Some good decisions will still have bad outcomes,

00:01:27.311 --> 00:01:29.909
and some weak decisions will get lucky.

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:33.620
Decision science gives you a way to keep improving anyway.
